## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming cardinality estimation under memory constraints—a fundamental skill for building analytics pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog**: Uses probabilistic counting to estimate cardinalities with sub-linear memory
- **Register updates**: Hash to determine bucket, count leading zeros, store maximum run length
- **Merge behavior**: Element-wise maximum of registers enables lossless parallel merging
- **Error bounds**: Relative standard error ≈ 1.04/√m; memory scales as O(m)

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 128) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  _hash(id) {
    let h = xmur3(id.toString());
    return splitmix64(h());
  }

  add(id) {
    const hash = this._hash(id);
    const idx = hash & (this.m - 1);
    const remaining = hash >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leadingZeros = Math.clz32(remaining) - (32 - Math.log2(this.m) - 1);
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leadingZeros + 1);
  }

  estimate() {
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, val) => acc + Math.pow(2, -val), 0);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }

  merge(other) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.m; i++) {
      this.registers[i] = Math.max(this.registers[i], other.registers[i]);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical sliding-window counting and maintaining top-k with bounded memory.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window data structure
- Hashmap for counts
- Top-k extraction in bounded time
- Deterministic tie-breaking

## Code Example
````javascript
// Sketch
const W = 10000;
const windowQ = [];
const counts = new Map();
function applyBatch(batch){
  for (const x of batch){
    windowQ.push(x);
    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);
    if (windowQ.length > W){
      const y = windowQ.shift();
      const ny = (counts.get(y) - 1);
      if (ny) counts.set(y, ny);
      else counts.delete(y);
    }
  }
  const top3 = [...counts.entries()].sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).slice(0,3).map(e=>e[0]);
  return top3;
}
````

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to a dynamic window size?
- What optimizations exist to avoid full re-scan on every batch?

